Object.assign(window.LESSON_CONTENT || (window.LESSON_CONTENT = {}), {"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w1":{"overview":"Students will begin the course by establishing safe and respectful computer-room routines and developing their understanding of problems and algorithms. They will break a familiar computing task into smaller parts and explain what each part needs to achieve. Students will then write precise, ordered instructions and improve them through partner testing, recognising why a computer requires more explicit instructions than a person may need.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Ask students to explain one subtask and show their correctly named saved file. Check order, termination and whether a partner can obtain the intended output. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"24 August 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to decompose a classroom computing task and follow safe working routines.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask how a computer-room task can go wrong without agreed routines. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain file naming, respectful use and decomposition into smaller tasks. Distinguish the overall problem from an individual task. Explain that decomposition helps a programmer organise work, locate errors and discuss responsibilities without trying to solve every detail at once."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Break preparing a class survey into input, processing and output tasks together. Ask students to identify how survey questions are collected, how answers are stored and how totals are displayed. Arrange their suggestions into a diagram and discuss which tasks depend on earlier results."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create a decomposition diagram for organising a class quiz; name each subtask and practise saving it. Students label the main problem and at least four subtasks. They exchange diagrams with a partner, explain the purpose of each part and amend a task that is too vague to implement."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain one subtask and show their correctly named saved file. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a main-problem box with two subtasks already shown. Ask what the user does, what the computer does and what result is needed. Demonstrate saving and reopening the file before students attempt it independently."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students divide a subtask into further steps and identify a dependency between two parts. Ask how separate programmers could develop the parts while agreeing the information exchanged."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"28 August 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to write an unambiguous algorithm with ordered steps.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Give deliberately vague instructions for drawing a shape. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain an algorithm as a finite, ordered method with a clear purpose. Discuss why a computer cannot infer missing steps from everyday experience. Introduce inputs and outputs and explain that instructions must specify what happens before the process ends."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Improve instructions for calculating the total price of two items. Use prices of 15 and 20 to follow the purchase algorithm. Ask where the values enter, where the total is calculated and how the customer receives the result. Rewrite an ambiguous instruction together."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Write an algorithm for a school purchase, then let a partner follow it literally and revise unclear steps. Students include a beginning, ordered processing steps and an identifiable output. Their partner records where an instruction could have more than one interpretation and checks the revised version using different prices."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check order, termination and whether a partner can obtain the intended output. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide an instruction bank and an input-process-output template. Model the first two steps, then ask students to arrange and complete the remaining instructions. Use oral rehearsal before they write the final algorithm."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify an assumption, such as whether prices can be negative, and explain how changing that assumption would affect the algorithm. They compare two correct sequences and justify their preference."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapters 1 and 4","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w2":{"overview":"Students will develop their ability to represent and follow algorithms using flowcharts, pseudocode and trace tables. They will identify the purpose of common flowchart symbols and represent a decision with clearly labelled branches. Students will then follow instructions in order, record changing variable values and use trace evidence to explain a result or locate an incorrect prediction.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check both branches and explain how the two representations match. Check every value change and require an explanation of the final output. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"31 August 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to represent a simple decision using a flowchart and pseudocode.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Compare a written recipe with a diagram of the same steps. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce start/end, input/output, process and decision symbols; use labelled branches. Explain that a flowchart shows control flow while pseudocode expresses steps without requiring a particular programming language. A decision needs a precise condition and an outcome for each possible answer."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Convert an age-based ticket decision into both representations. Use a pass mark of 50 and trace scores of 49, 50 and 51. Ask students to choose the correct symbol for each step and label the true and false routes before writing equivalent pseudocode."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Draw a flowchart and write matching pseudocode for deciding whether a score reaches a pass threshold. Students include input, a comparison, two clearly labelled outputs and an end point. Pairs trace the same three scores through both representations and correct any mismatch between the diagram and the written steps."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check both branches and explain how the two representations match. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide the flowchart symbols with their meanings and a partially completed decision. Use arrows and a condition word bank. Read the pseudocode aloud while students point to the matching flowchart step."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students add a permitted score range and explain how an invalid value should be handled before the pass decision. They justify why the order of the decisions matters."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"4 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to trace an algorithm and record changes in variable values.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Predict the output of a short number-swapping algorithm. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain trace-table columns and updating values one instruction at a time. Explain that a trace table records execution rather than guesses about the intended result. Only update a value when an instruction changes it; the order of assignment statements therefore matters."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Trace a total and a discount decision using two supplied inputs. Use a price of 40 and quantity of 3, then apply a discount when the total exceeds 100. Record the total before and after the decision and compare it with a case that receives no discount."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Complete trace tables for two short algorithms, then identify and correct one incorrect predicted output. Students show the initial inputs, each changed value, the branch followed and the final output. They use the completed trace to explain the faulty prediction and rewrite it with a reason."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check every value change and require an explanation of the final output. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide named columns, line numbers and the first completed row. Highlight one instruction at a time and allow students to use value cards before transferring the changes into their trace table."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students create an input that follows the other branch and explain why its output differs. They identify a redundant table entry and distinguish unchanged values from uninitialised values."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapters 1 and 2","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w3":{"overview":"Students will become familiar with the Python editing environment and the process of writing, saving, running and correcting a short program. They will use comments and meaningful identifiers to improve readability. Students will also develop their understanding of assignment, variables and basic data types, predicting how stored values change and distinguishing numeric values from text and Boolean values.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check saved source code, successful output and one explained readability choice. Ask students to identify each type and explain the final score. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"7 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to run and save a short Python program using readable code.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a program with an unclear name and unreadable layout. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce the editor, running code, comments and meaningful identifiers. Explain the difference between editing source code and running it. Discuss meaningful filenames, indentation, comments and readable identifiers as ways of making a program easier to understand and maintain."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Write, save and run a greeting program; demonstrate a syntax error and its correction. Type a short greeting, save it as a Python file and show how the output differs from the code. Remove a quotation mark, read the error message and explain why correcting syntax is different from changing the intended greeting."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create a named greeting program with an appropriate comment, then reopen and run the saved file. Students submit the saved source file and demonstrate that it runs after reopening. A partner reads the code and identifies its purpose, the output statement and the comment that helps explain it."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check saved source code, successful output and one explained readability choice. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a short code template and a visual save-run checklist. Identify the editor and output areas on the display. Guide students through matching quotation marks, then remove the template for a second attempt."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students improve a deliberately unclear version without altering its output and justify each change. They distinguish a useful explanatory comment from a comment that merely repeats the code."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"11 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use variables and distinguish integer float string and Boolean values.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Compare 12, 12.5, \"12\" and True. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain assignment, changing a value and the four basic data types. Explain that assignment stores a value under a name and that a later assignment replaces the previous value. Compare a numeric value with a string containing digits and discuss when a Boolean value is useful."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Predict and run assignments that update a player score. Set score to 10, add 5 and then double it. Ask students to predict each value before running the code. Use type() to inspect an integer, a decimal value, a player name and a completion flag."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create variables for a game player, score, time and completion flag; update the score and display values. Students produce a small table of variable names, initial values, types and final values. They explain why the player name is a string and show that updating the score does not change the other variables."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to identify each type and explain the final score. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide examples of the four data types and a partially completed value table. Use a labelled storage-box analogy for assignment and ask students to explain one update orally before coding it."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students investigate the difference between 12 and \"12\" in an expression. They predict and explain a type-related error and propose a suitable conversion without changing the meaning of the data."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapters 5 and 6","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w4":{"overview":"Students will develop interactive programs that collect information and present clear output. They will explain the purpose of prompts and recognise that input() returns a string. Students will convert numeric input when calculation is required and use a supplied decision structure to check a permitted range. They will distinguish validation from verification and recognise that conversion alone does not make data valid.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check prompts, stored input and a correct summary for two trials. Check 0, 100 and an out-of-range number; explain why valid data can still be inaccurate. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"14 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to collect input and produce meaningful output in Python.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask why a program needs a prompt before accepting input. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain input(), print() and that input() returns a string. Discuss the purpose of an informative prompt and explain how the returned string is stored for later use. Distinguish input from output and avoid collecting real personal information during classroom practice."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Build an interactive name greeting with clear prompts and output. Ask for a fictional club member and favourite activity. Store the responses separately and print a readable confirmation. Run the program with different responses and ask which parts of the output change."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create a short club-registration dialogue using fictional names and display a readable summary. Students include two clear prompts, meaningful variable names and a complete confirmation message. They test two fictional registrations and ask a partner whether the prompts make the expected response clear."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check prompts, stored input and a correct summary for two trials. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a prompt-variable-output planning sheet and a working single-input example. Colour-code the stored response and its later use. Students add the second input after explaining the first example."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students improve the layout of the confirmation without duplicating input statements. They explain why an empty response is still a string and suggest a requirement for handling it."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"18 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to convert numeric input and check a simple permitted range.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Predict what happens when two input strings are added. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain int(), float() and range checks; distinguish validation from verifying accuracy. Explain why converting a numeric string permits arithmetic, while a range check decides whether the resulting value is permitted. Validating data does not prove that the user entered the factually correct value."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Convert a supplied numeric score and check whether it lies between 0 and 100. Use a score of 50 to demonstrate int() and compare the result with the original string. Show a supplied range-checking structure, then trace 0, 100 and 101 through it. Discuss non-numeric input as a limitation to address later."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Build a score checker using numeric test inputs; reject values outside 0–100. Record expected results first. Students use the supplied decision structure to complete the checker and record expected and actual results. Include both valid endpoints and at least one invalid numeric value; explain one difference between conversion and validation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check 0, 100 and an out-of-range number; explain why valid data can still be inaccurate. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a working range-check template because independent selection is developed later. Model substituting the score into each comparison and use a number line to show the inclusive valid interval."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare int() and float() for a decimal score and explain which matches the stated requirement. They identify the risk of non-numeric input and describe an appropriate error message in plain language."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapters 5 and 9","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w5":{"overview":"Students will develop their understanding of computational thinking through decomposition and abstraction. They will learn how to break a complex problem into smaller, manageable sub-problems and how abstraction can be used to remove unnecessary detail when designing a solution. Students will also identify inputs, outputs, processing and initialisation requirements when analysing a problem and apply these techniques to a computer-based problem.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed problem-solving activity. Students should be able to explain decomposition and abstraction in their own words, identify sub-problems within a larger problem and distinguish between input, output and processing requirements when analysing a problem.","lessons":[{"date":"21 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain computational thinking, decomposition and abstraction. They will be able to break a complex problem into smaller sub-problems and identify why decomposition makes a problem easier to solve.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present a familiar complex problem, such as creating a computer version of a game. Ask: “If we had to program this entire game, could we solve everything at once?” Ask students to suggest smaller tasks that would need to be completed."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce computational thinking and explain that computer scientists define and analyse problems before creating algorithms and programs. Introduce decomposition as breaking a problem into smaller, more manageable sub-problems. Explain that decomposition can make errors easier to identify and allows different parts of a problem to be worked on separately."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Examine the textbook's noughts and crosses example. Identify the different sub-problems, including designing the 3×3 interface, tracking selected squares, deciding the computer's move and checking when the game is over. Discuss how solving these smaller problems contributes to solving the complete problem."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students choose a familiar computer-based problem and decompose it into at least four smaller sub-problems. They should give a short description of what needs to be solved for each sub-problem."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask selected students to share their decomposed problems. Exit check: students explain what decomposition means and give one benefit of using it when solving a programming problem."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a partially completed decomposition example and guide students through identifying the main problem and its smaller parts. Use the noughts and crosses example as a model and provide prompts such as “What does the user need to do?”, “What does the computer need to do?” and “How will the program know when it is finished?”"},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students decompose a more complex problem into a greater number of sub-problems and explain how different programmers or subprograms could work on the separate parts. Students who finish confidently can identify which sub-problems may need to interact with each other."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"25 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain abstraction and identify the essential details required to model a real-world problem. They will identify inputs, outputs, processing and initialisation requirements when designing a solution.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a real-world object or situation, such as a die or a journey. Ask: “If we wanted to create a computer model of this, would we need every detail of the real object?” Discuss which details are important and which could be ignored."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce abstraction as removing or hiding unnecessary detail so that only the important points remain. Explain how programmers use abstraction when creating algorithms and models. Discuss the textbook's virtual die example and how only the essential behaviour of a real die needs to be represented."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Examine the noughts and crosses example and identify its inputs, outputs, processing and initialisation requirements. Discuss how a programmer can work at a high level of abstraction before adding more detail. Introduce the idea of using separate subprograms for specific tasks."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students analyse the textbook's Creating a Quiz Game activity. In groups or pairs, they list the requirements, decompose the problem into sub-problems and identify the inputs, outputs and processing requirements. Students should also describe how the spinning wheel could be modelled."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask groups to present one part of their analysis. Exit check: students define abstraction in their own words and identify one input, one output and one processing requirement from the quiz game."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a template divided into Problem → Sub-problems → Inputs → Outputs → Processing. Model one example before students begin. Provide vocabulary prompts for abstraction, input, output, processing, initialisation and subprogram."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify different levels of abstraction within the quiz game and explain which details can be hidden at a higher level. Students who finish confidently can write simple pseudocode describing how the wheel could select one of the four colours."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Student Book / textbook pages 23–28","Whiteboard / display screen","Noughts and crosses example from the textbook","Quiz Game activity from the textbook","Problem decomposition worksheet / notebook","Pseudocode template","Computers, if students are completing the programming extension"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w6":{"overview":"Students will develop their understanding of selection and the Boolean conditions used to control it. They will evaluate comparisons and combine conditions to describe a permitted range. Students will then use if, elif and else to classify values, explaining the importance of indentation and branch order. They will use values at and around thresholds to check that the program implements the intended rules.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check equality at the threshold and explain why each test is True or False. Require correct boundary results and an explanation of branch order. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"28 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to construct and evaluate Boolean conditions for selection.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask whether a score equal to the pass mark should pass. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain comparison operators and Boolean results; introduce and, or and not. Explain equality and inequality comparisons and show that each comparison evaluates to True or False. Discuss how and requires both conditions to be true, while or permits either condition to be true."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Evaluate conditions for eligibility based on age and permission. Use a permitted age interval of 12 to 18 and compare a correct and condition with an incorrect or condition. Substitute ages of 11, 12, 18 and 19 and explain the result of each comparison."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Write and test conditions for a score threshold and a permitted age range. Students record conditions and predicted Boolean results before running them. They test the endpoints and outside values, then explain why a condition that appears reasonable may accept an unintended value."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check equality at the threshold and explain why each test is True or False. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a comparison-operator reference and number-line diagrams. Start with one condition before combining two. Ask students to read each expression as a complete sentence and explain each Boolean result."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students use not to express the opposite condition and check it against the original interval test. They identify a redundant condition and justify a simpler equivalent expression."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"2 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use if elif and else to select one suitable outcome.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show overlapping grade rules in an incorrect order. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain indentation and why the first true branch is selected. Explain that Python evaluates if and elif conditions in order and executes the first matching branch. Indentation defines the statements belonging to a branch; else handles the remaining cases."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Build a three-band score classifier using ordered conditions. Use three score bands: below 50, 50–79 and 80 or above. Trace scores of 49, 50, 79 and 80. Show how putting a broad condition first can prevent a more specific branch from being reached."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create and test the classifier with values below, at and above each threshold. Students save their classifier with labelled output and a test table. They record the expected band for each boundary case, compare actual results and correct either the comparison or the order when necessary."},{"label":"C:","text":"Require correct boundary results and an explanation of branch order. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a branch diagram and an indented code skeleton. Complete the first comparison together and supply the band labels. Use the same boundary values in the diagram, code and test table."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two correct arrangements of the conditions and explain why each works. They add a preliminary range check for 0–100 and justify where it belongs."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapter 5","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w7":{"overview":"Students will consolidate their understanding of algorithms, tracing, variables, input and output, numeric conversion, selection, decomposition and abstraction through a mock assessment and a structured review of their responses. They will use feedback to identify the cause of an error, correct their work and apply the same idea to a different example. The review will prepare students for the internal school assessment by making both their strengths and their remaining learning needs clear.","assessment":"Compare the original mock responses, annotated corrections and independent follow-up work. Students should be able to explain why an answer or program was incorrect, show a justified correction and apply the same skill to a different question. Use individual discussions and test evidence to identify remaining misconceptions before assessment week.","lessons":[{"date":"5 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to complete a mock task and identify gaps in taught skills.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Recall the taught success criteria before opening the task. Ensure that students have the correct task sheet and understand the purpose of this lesson before beginning."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the mock format and distinguish independent work from later feedback. Discuss how marks or success criteria distinguish a correct final result from a clear explanation. Remind students that the mock is used to find learning needs before the school assessment."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Read the instructions together without solving the assessed items. Use the task cover sheet to identify the required evidence: a trace table, an explanation and a saved source file with tests. Check understanding of the instructions without solving the mock questions."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Complete a short tracing task and build a score classifier with input, arithmetic and selection; record expected results before running it. Students work independently before discussing answers. They mark any uncertain step and keep their original response so that the later correction shows what changed and why."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the mock work and identify one specific error or uncertainty for correction. Record the specific outcome demonstrated and any concept that requires follow-up, using the agreed success criteria."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the misconception identified in their mock work. Provide a targeted worked example, vocabulary prompts or a partially completed trace table during the review, then remove the scaffold for the follow-up question."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify an alternative solution, design a test that exposes a plausible error and explain why it is effective. Ask them to compare the clarity of two correct approaches using the stated success criteria."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"9 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to correct misunderstandings and apply feedback to a new task.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Discuss a common anonymous error from the mock. Ensure that students have the correct task sheet and understand the purpose of this lesson before beginning."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the reason for the error and the relevant rule. Distinguish a syntax error, a logic error and an incomplete explanation using anonymous examples from the mock. Explain that correcting the visible answer is not enough unless the reason for the error is understood."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Correct one similar example together and explain each change. Model a similar example rather than simply giving the original answer. Compare the incorrect reasoning with the corrected approach and show how a new test confirms the change."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Correct personal mock work, then solve a new example independently and rerun relevant tests. Students annotate each correction with its cause and complete a new question testing the same skill. They rerun the relevant program cases and record one specific action to improve their assessment preparation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Compare the new response with the original and record the remaining learning need. Record the specific outcome demonstrated and any concept that requires follow-up, using the agreed success criteria."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the misconception identified in their mock work. Provide a targeted worked example, vocabulary prompts or a partially completed trace table during the review, then remove the scaffold for the follow-up question."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify an alternative solution, design a test that exposes a plausible error and explain why it is effective. Ask them to compare the clarity of two correct approaches using the stated success criteria."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapters 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 9","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared mock assessment tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w8":{"overview":"Students will demonstrate their understanding of algorithms, tracing, variables, input and output, numeric conversion, selection, decomposition and abstraction through internal school knowledge and practical assessments. They will interpret problems, trace instructions and produce independent responses. The practical lesson will collect evidence of their ability to construct a working Python solution, test it against stated requirements and explain the results. Assessment will focus on the content and skills taught during the term.","assessment":"Use the prepared school marking criteria, individual written responses, runnable source files and completed test tables. Check correctness, the quality of explanations and whether the stated requirements are met. Record authorised support and identify particular knowledge or programming skills that need further teaching after assessment.","lessons":[{"date":"12 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate the taught problem-solving skills independently.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Settle students and check the school access arrangements. Ensure that students have the correct task sheet and understand the purpose of this lesson before beginning."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain task instructions, permitted resources and how evidence will be collected. Explain that the knowledge tasks cover the taught concepts and require individual evidence. Make clear which resources are permitted and how an explanation should be recorded; use the agreed school assessment arrangements."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Confirm students understand how to record answers without demonstrating solutions. Check that every student can access the question paper and understands where to record answers. Read administrative instructions as needed without interpreting the problem or suggesting a method."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Complete the internal school knowledge and tracing assessment on decomposition, algorithms, tracing, variables, input/output, arithmetic and selection. Students complete the prepared questions independently. Collect all work, retain any trace tables and record the nature of authorised access support so that performance can be interpreted fairly."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect individual responses and record access support without coaching assessed answers. Record the specific outcome demonstrated and any concept that requires follow-up, using the agreed success criteria."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply the student’s agreed school access arrangements and clarify administrative instructions only. Record any assistance provided. Do not supply code, complete trace-table entries or explain a solution to an assessed problem."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same taught requirements, students justify their choices and provide additional relevant test evidence. Assess the accuracy of their reasoning and the quality of the solution without introducing an untaught topic during assessment."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"16 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate the taught Python skills in an independent practical task.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Check the editor and individual assessment save folder. Ensure that students have the correct task sheet and understand the purpose of this lesson before beginning."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Read the practical requirements and explain the submission process. Explain that the saved program and its test evidence will be assessed against the stated requirements. Students should retain working files and record expected results before testing, rather than changing predictions after seeing the output."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Clarify administrative questions only; do not model the assessed solution. Check access to Python and the individual submission folder. Clarify filenames, the required evidence and the submission procedure without demonstrating code that solves the assessment task."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Independently build a score classifier with input, arithmetic and selection, save source code and provide expected and actual test results. Students build and test their own solution, saving the source and a test table. Where a requirement remains unmet, they identify the limitation honestly and submit the evidence of what their program does."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect runnable code and test evidence; assess correctness, explanation and independence against taught criteria. Record the specific outcome demonstrated and any concept that requires follow-up, using the agreed success criteria."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply the student’s agreed school access arrangements and clarify administrative instructions only. Record any assistance provided. Do not supply code, complete trace-table entries or explain a solution to an assessed problem."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same taught requirements, students justify their choices and provide additional relevant test evidence. Assess the accuracy of their reasoning and the quality of the solution without introducing an untaught topic during assessment."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapters 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 9","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared internal school assessment tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w10":{"overview":"Students will develop their understanding of count-controlled repetition and use for loops to replace repeated instructions. They will explain the values produced by range() and use a loop to generate a multiplication table. Students will then trace an accumulator, recognise the importance of initialisation and use test evidence to identify and correct an off-by-one error.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check exactly ten results and explain the start and stop values. Check the final total and explain the change needed to include the endpoint. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"26 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use a for loop with range to repeat a task a known number of times.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Compare five repeated print statements with one loop. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain count-controlled repetition and the excluded stop value in range(). Explain when the number of repetitions is known before a loop starts. Connect the values produced by range() to the number of loop iterations and show that the stop value itself is excluded."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Predict and run a loop that displays numbers 1 to 5. Use range(1, 6) and record the loop variable before each displayed result. Change the stop value and ask students to predict the number of lines before running the revised version."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create a multiplication-table program for a chosen number using a loop from 1 to 10. Students use one loop to generate ten labelled multiplication facts. They first predict the results for a chosen number and then check the first, last and total number of displayed lines."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check exactly ten results and explain the start and stop values. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide the loop header and an indented print statement with one missing expression. Use a table of loop-variable values and products. Students explain why range(1, 11) is used before removing the scaffold."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students add a different start or step value and predict the resulting facts. They explain how the number of iterations changes and distinguish the loop variable from the chosen multiplier."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"30 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to trace a for loop and test for off-by-one errors.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a loop that produces only nine results. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain iteration, loop-variable updates and accumulating a total. Explain that an accumulator needs an initial value before the loop and is updated during each iteration. An off-by-one error often comes from misunderstanding the inclusive start or excluded stop value."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Trace a running total for the integers 1 to 4. Trace total = 0 followed by a loop adding 1, 2, 3 and 4. Record both the loop variable and running total, then compare a faulty stop value that omits the final number."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Trace and repair a total-calculation program; test one iteration and several iterations. Students repair the endpoint and complete a trace table for the corrected program. They test a single-value interval and a longer interval, documenting the expected sum and the actual result for each."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check the final total and explain the change needed to include the endpoint. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide the accumulator initialisation and the first trace row. Use counters to represent each added value and highlight the difference between the current loop variable and the accumulated total."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students investigate what happens if the total is reset inside the loop and explain the incorrect result. They justify the placement of initialisation using their trace evidence."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapter 5","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist; confirm Friday school-event arrangements and reschedule any displaced task"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w11":{"overview":"Students will develop their understanding of condition-controlled repetition and explain how a while loop can execute zero or more times. They will trace a changing condition and identify what allows the loop to terminate. Students will then combine repetition and selection in a text menu, providing useful feedback for an unknown choice and a clear exit option.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check termination and explain the zero-iteration case. Test a valid option, an invalid option and quit; check that the menu does not become trapped. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"2 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use a while loop with a condition that changes.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Predict what happens when a loop counter never changes. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain pre-condition testing, initialisation and updating to allow termination. Explain that a while loop tests its condition before the body executes. A loop may therefore run zero times. The program must eventually change the relevant value or provide another clear way to finish."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Trace a countdown including its initial condition and final value. Start a countdown at 3, display the value and reduce it by 1. Trace the condition before each iteration and after reaching 0. Contrast this with starting at 0 and with omitting the update."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Write a countdown and trace a case where the loop body never runs. Students submit their countdown and traces for a positive start and a zero start. They identify the statement responsible for progress and explain why the condition eventually becomes false."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check termination and explain the zero-iteration case. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a condition-update diagram and a complete countdown with one missing line. Trace values on the whiteboard before coding. Use a stop-and-check routine when students suspect an endless loop."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students change the stopping condition and predict the final displayed value. They explain when a for loop would express the same task more simply and when while is more suitable."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"6 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to build a repeating menu with a clear exit option.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask what should happen after an unknown menu choice. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain a sentinel choice and selection inside a while loop. Explain that a menu repeats until a sentinel choice is entered and that selection determines the action taken during each repetition. An unknown choice should give feedback and return safely to the menu."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a text menu with one information option and a quit choice. Use choices 1 for information and Q for quit. Trace the sequence 1, X, Q and show when the choice variable is updated. Discuss why reading a new choice only before the loop can prevent the program from exiting."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create a two-action menu that repeats, handles an unknown choice and exits when requested. Students add a second useful text action, keep the menu readable and save the program. A partner tests valid options, an unknown option and quit in one session and records whether each response meets the requirements."},{"label":"C:","text":"Test a valid option, an invalid option and quit; check that the menu does not become trapped. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a menu code skeleton with clear places for input, selection and repetition. Use a flow diagram to locate the next input. Model one option and let students complete the remaining branches."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students normalise the quit choice only if they can explain the string operation used, or document case sensitivity as a limitation. They justify their response to an unknown menu option."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapter 5","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w12":{"overview":"Students will develop their ability to inspect and process text in Python. They will identify character positions, use string length and select sections of text using slicing. Students will then apply string methods to normalise responses before comparing them. They will test empty input, case differences and surrounding spaces, explaining both the benefits and limitations of their solution.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check correct indices and explain how the program avoids indexing an empty string. Test uppercase, lowercase, spaces and empty input; explain one limitation of the checker. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"9 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use string length indexing and slicing safely.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask which position contains the first character in Python. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain zero-based indexing, len() and the excluded stop position in a slice. Explain that Python strings contain characters with zero-based positions. The length counts all characters, including spaces, while a slice selects a sequence up to but not including its stop position."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Predict the results of indexing and slicing a supplied word. Use the word COMPUTER to label positions 0 to 7. Predict word[0], word[1:4] and len(word), then run them. Show why requesting the first character of an empty string is unsafe."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Write a program that displays a word length, its first character and a selected slice; check for empty input first. Students produce a small table of expressions, predictions and actual results. Their program checks for an empty response before accessing a character, and they explain which positions are included in their chosen slice."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check correct indices and explain how the program avoids indexing an empty string. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide an indexed character strip and partially completed expression table. Model counting positions from zero and shade the characters in a slice. Supply the empty-input guard before independent practice."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare a slice extending beyond the string with an out-of-range single index. They describe the observed difference and propose a test that demonstrates it clearly."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"13 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to apply string operations to normalise and inspect text.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Compare \"  YES \" with \"yes\" in a decision. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain strip(), lower() and membership tests without changing the original string in place. Explain that normalisation makes comparable responses consistent and that string methods return new strings. Discuss when removing surrounding spaces and changing case are useful and when the original text should be retained."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Build a fictional club-code checker using a normalised input. Store the original response \"  YES \" and a normalised version using strip().lower(). Compare the two and use a condition to recognise an accepted response. Demonstrate that an empty normalised response needs separate feedback."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create a response checker that accepts mixed-case input with surrounding spaces and reports empty input. Students define accepted responses before coding and test mixed case, surrounding spaces, an incorrect response and empty input. They keep expected and actual results and explain which requirement each string operation supports."},{"label":"C:","text":"Test uppercase, lowercase, spaces and empty input; explain one limitation of the checker. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a before-and-after table for strip() and lower(). Separate the method calls into two assignments before combining them. Give a response list so students focus on processing rather than inventing rules."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students test a response containing internal spaces and explain why strip() does not remove them. They describe a limitation without claiming the checker can interpret any natural-language answer."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapter 7","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w13":{"overview":"Students will develop a systematic approach to testing and evaluating programs against stated requirements. They will record expected and actual results and use this evidence to identify and correct a logic error. Students will select normal, boundary and invalid test data, justify how each case checks a requirement and distinguish a successful test from a claim that the whole program is correct.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, discussion of worked examples, the completed student tasks and the final checks in both lessons. Check evidence before and after the correction and one justified evaluation statement. Require expected and actual outcomes and explain how a test exposes an incorrect comparison. Check that students can explain their reasoning and apply the skill without relying on the worked example.","lessons":[{"date":"16 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to distinguish testing from evaluation and record useful test evidence.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a working program that still does not meet its stated purpose. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain expected and actual results, error diagnosis and evaluation against requirements. Distinguish showing that a program runs from showing that it meets its requirements. Explain how a repeatable test records its purpose, input, expected result, actual result and whether the requirement is satisfied."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Test a score classifier and link each case to a stated requirement. Use a classifier that awards a pass only above 50 even though the requirement says 50 or above. Predict the outcome for 50, run the test and correct the comparison. Rerun an ordinary case to check the change."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Create a test table, run the supplied program and correct a discovered logic error. Students produce a completed test table and keep the faulty and corrected results. Their evaluation refers to a stated requirement, cites the evidence and identifies one remaining limitation or improvement."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check evidence before and after the correction and one justified evaluation statement. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide the requirements and a test-table template with one example completed. Highlight the difference between expected and actual results. Guide students to describe the observed error before changing the code."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain why rerunning only the previously failing case is insufficient. They choose an additional case that checks whether the correction has introduced another error."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"20 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to select normal boundary and invalid test data for a program.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask why testing only a score of 50 misses important errors. Invite students to explain their prediction before demonstrating the example. Use their responses to identify the idea that needs clarification."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain normal values, valid limits and invalid values outside the accepted range. Explain that normal data lies within the expected range, boundary data checks the permitted limits and invalid data violates the rule. Some conventions also group values immediately outside a limit with boundary testing; always state the rule and expected outcome."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Select 0, 100, -1 and 101 for a permitted range of 0–100. For an inclusive 0–100 range, choose 50 as normal, 0 and 100 as valid boundaries, and -1 and 101 as invalid numeric values. Identify text as another invalid category requiring a different handling strategy."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Test a range-checking program using normal, boundary and invalid numeric data; identify text input as a further limitation. Students justify each selected test, run the checker and record the output. They modify a faulty comparison and show which test detects the error; document text input as an unresolved limitation if conversion handling is not implemented."},{"label":"C:","text":"Require expected and actual outcomes and explain how a test exposes an incorrect comparison. Ask students to support their answer with a specific example from their work, and record any misconception that needs a further demonstration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a labelled number line and prepared test values to sort. Model one expected result and give sentence stems linking a value to the requirement. Support the recording process without choosing every test for students."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students add a decimal case and explain whether it is valid under an integer-only requirement. They distinguish testing a validation rule from proving that an entered score is factually correct."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Chapter 11","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared algorithm, tracing or Python practice tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w14":{"overview":"Students will consolidate their understanding of algorithms, tracing, variables, input and output, numeric conversion, selection, decomposition and abstraction, repetition, string processing and testing through a mock assessment and a structured review of their responses. They will use feedback to identify the cause of an error, correct their work and apply the same idea to a different example. The review will prepare students for the internal school assessment by making both their strengths and their remaining learning needs clear.","assessment":"Compare the original mock responses, annotated corrections and independent follow-up work. Students should be able to explain why an answer or program was incorrect, show a justified correction and apply the same skill to a different question. Use individual discussions and test evidence to identify remaining misconceptions before assessment week.","lessons":[{"date":"23 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to complete a mock task and identify gaps in taught skills.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Recall the taught success criteria before opening the task. Ensure that students have the correct task sheet and understand the purpose of this lesson before beginning."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the mock format and distinguish independent work from later feedback. Discuss how marks or success criteria distinguish a correct final result from a clear explanation. Remind students that the mock is used to find learning needs before the school assessment."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Read the instructions together without solving the assessed items. Use the task cover sheet to identify the required evidence: a trace table, an explanation and a saved source file with tests. Check understanding of the instructions without solving the mock questions."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Complete a short tracing task and build a repeating text menu using selection, a loop and string normalisation; record expected results before running it. Students work independently before discussing answers. They mark any uncertain step and keep their original response so that the later correction shows what changed and why."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the mock work and identify one specific error or uncertainty for correction. Record the specific outcome demonstrated and any concept that requires follow-up, using the agreed success criteria."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the misconception identified in their mock work. Provide a targeted worked example, vocabulary prompts or a partially completed trace table during the review, then remove the scaffold for the follow-up question."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify an alternative solution, design a test that exposes a plausible error and explain why it is effective. Ask them to compare the clarity of two correct approaches using the stated success criteria."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"27 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to correct misunderstandings and apply feedback to a new task.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Discuss a common anonymous error from the mock. Ensure that students have the correct task sheet and understand the purpose of this lesson before beginning."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the reason for the error and the relevant rule. Distinguish a syntax error, a logic error and an incomplete explanation using anonymous examples from the mock. Explain that correcting the visible answer is not enough unless the reason for the error is understood."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Correct one similar example together and explain each change. Model a similar example rather than simply giving the original answer. Compare the incorrect reasoning with the corrected approach and show how a new test confirms the change."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Correct personal mock work, then solve a new example independently and rerun relevant tests. Students annotate each correction with its cause and complete a new question testing the same skill. They rerun the relevant program cases and record one specific action to improve their assessment preparation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Compare the new response with the original and record the remaining learning need. Record the specific outcome demonstrated and any concept that requires follow-up, using the agreed success criteria."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the misconception identified in their mock work. Provide a targeted worked example, vocabulary prompts or a partially completed trace table during the review, then remove the scaffold for the follow-up question."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify an alternative solution, design a test that exposes a plausible error and explain why it is effective. Ask them to compare the clarity of two correct approaches using the stated success criteria."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel International GCSE Computer Science Student Book: Problem Solving and Programming units: taught topics only","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared mock assessment tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success criteria, test tables and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-computer-science-t1-w15":{"overview":"Students will demonstrate their understanding of algorithms, tracing, variables, input and output, numeric conversion, selection, decomposition and abstraction, repetition, string processing and testing through internal school knowledge and practical assessments. They will interpret problems, trace instructions and produce independent responses. The practical lesson will collect evidence of their ability to construct a working Python solution, test it against stated requirements and explain the results. Assessment will focus on the content and skills taught during the term.","assessment":"Use the prepared school marking criteria, individual written responses, runnable source files and completed test tables. Check correctness, the quality of explanations and whether the stated requirements are met. Record authorised support and identify particular knowledge or programming skills that need further teaching after assessment.","lessons":[{"date":"30 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate the taught problem-solving skills independently.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Settle students and check the school access arrangements."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain task instructions, permitted resources and how evidence will be collected. Explain that the knowledge tasks cover the taught concepts and require individual evidence. Make clear which resources are permitted and how an explanation should be recorded; use the agreed school assessment arrangements."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Confirm students understand how to record answers without demonstrating solutions. Check that every student can access the question paper and understands where to record answers. Read administrative instructions as needed without interpreting the problem or suggesting a method."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Complete the internal school knowledge and tracing assessment on decomposition, algorithms, tracing, variables, input/output, arithmetic and selection, loops, strings and testing. Students complete the prepared questions independently. Collect all work, retain any trace tables and record the nature of authorised access support so that performance can be interpreted fairly."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect individual responses and record access support without coaching assessed answers."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply the student’s agreed school access arrangements and clarify administrative instructions only. Record any assistance provided. Do not supply code, complete trace-table entries or explain a solution to an assessed problem."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same taught requirements, students justify their choices and provide additional relevant test evidence. Assess the accuracy of their reasoning and the quality of the solution without introducing an untaught topic during assessment."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"4 December 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate the taught Python skills in an independent practical task.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Check the editor and individual assessment save folder."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Read the practical requirements and explain the submission process. Explain that the saved program and its test evidence will be assessed against the stated requirements. Students should retain working files and record expected results before testing, rather than changing predictions after seeing the output."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Clarify administrative questions only; do not model the assessed solution. Check access to Python and the individual submission folder. Clarify filenames, the required evidence and the submission procedure without demonstrating code that solves the assessment task."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Independently build a repeating text menu using selection, a loop and string normalisation, save source code and provide expected and actual test results. Students build and test their own solution, saving the source and a test table. Where a requirement remains unmet, they identify the limitation honestly and submit the evidence of what their program does."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect runnable code and test evidence; assess correctness, explanation and independence against taught criteria."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply the student’s agreed school access arrangements and clarify administrative instructions only. Record any assistance provided. Do not supply code, complete trace-table entries or explain a solution to an assessed problem."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same taught requirements, students justify their choices and provide additional relevant test evidence. Assess the accuracy of their reasoning and the quality of the solution without introducing an untaught topic during assessment."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel Computer Science: taught Problem Solving and Programming topics","Teacher computer and classroom display","Classroom computers with Python 3 editor and individual save folders","Prepared internal school assessment tasks aligned with the lesson objectives","Whiteboard and markers","Student notebooks and pencils","Success checklist; confirm Friday event arrangements and reschedule displaced assessment"]}});
